#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535305 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b7ecb05-1f77-5c9b-949f-8b550e12bba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^{4} \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535312} {:answer/id #uuid "5f024063-ee92-5cb7-978b-c79d51157378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^{3} \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535316} {:answer/id #uuid "64e43474-9757-5e33-b010-824d5d3f6779" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{2 \\sqrt{2}}r^{4} \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535317} {:answer/id #uuid "b084bb87-f29b-5c6f-94e2-128e3b06e91d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{4}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^{4} \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535328} {:answer/id #uuid "e7d518f6-4dab-5351-89ef-0d2c20254b60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^{5} \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535339}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b7ecb05-1f77-5c9b-949f-8b550e12bba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^{4} \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535312} :answer-field/id #uuid "a94d5ba0-df8c-591a-8338-f599fb3218e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535326}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "304130fc-d60c-53ec-8352-d4d3005586d2" :question/problem "![](images/38/38947ad6e5f10e1e25d3d10777ad28c47aac1f0c0f311621c0194bef815331ab.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the quarter-circle shown above. What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_D 2 x y \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n$$ expressed in polar coordinates?\n\nChoose a change-of-variables setup with $r\\geq0$ that describes $D$ and the transformed integrand, including the Jacobian." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\leq r\\leq \\sqrt{2},\\ \\pi\\leq\\theta\\leq \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nSince $r\\geq0$, $\\sqrt{r^2}=r$; also $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$.\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 2 x y \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A&=\\iint_\\Delta 2 (r\\cos\\theta) (r\\sin\\theta) \\sqrt{(r\\cos\\theta)^{2} + (r\\sin\\theta)^{2}}\\,r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}2r^3\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta\\sqrt{r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}2r^4\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{\\pi}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^4\\sin(2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535318 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "266130f7-fe7a-555d-8bc3-c644a951aa45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^{4} \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535303} {:answer/id #uuid "33180dbf-afc0-5cf5-a045-d20f9c205b3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^{3} \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535306} {:answer/id #uuid "a958bcf2-9156-5ac1-892f-0a551baa748b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{2 \\sqrt{2}}r^{4} \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535327} {:answer/id #uuid "d0307e5f-4a08-5149-9fb2-d9fdd6da4411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^{4} \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535334} {:answer/id #uuid "eb76449e-7e26-50e6-90fa-e3ea350c8e4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^{5} \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535340}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0307e5f-4a08-5149-9fb2-d9fdd6da4411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^{4} \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535334} :answer-field/id #uuid "416649c6-1d29-50f8-997b-94939fdfa192" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "65d15c33-14f0-5a8d-980c-cdd3dc5eab53" :question/problem "![](images/b4/b4a332a1bea58e47ab58fa47c6944213ce4caa5d26e6bfb129fea613adcd524b.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the quarter-circle shown above. What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_D 2 x y \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n$$ expressed in polar coordinates?\n\nChoose a change-of-variables setup with $r\\geq0$ that describes $D$ and the transformed integrand, including the Jacobian." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\leq r\\leq \\sqrt{2},\\ 0\\leq\\theta\\leq \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nSince $r\\geq0$, $\\sqrt{r^2}=r$; also $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$.\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 2 x y \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A&=\\iint_\\Delta 2 (r\\cos\\theta) (r\\sin\\theta) \\sqrt{(r\\cos\\theta)^{2} + (r\\sin\\theta)^{2}}\\,r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}2r^3\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta\\sqrt{r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}2r^4\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^4\\sin(2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535320 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "216605b1-8c00-525a-9c5d-7a682e81d7b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^{4} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535302} {:answer/id #uuid "89118a1c-8cc3-5bd9-ac73-77a6bba3f97c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^{4} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535323} {:answer/id #uuid "b6049116-ddb2-5a7e-a166-ed4e50566d53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^{3} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535331} {:answer/id #uuid "d8d4929e-7e22-5c5e-a9ba-ea7f31e3d282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^{5} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535336} {:answer/id #uuid "f7b6a449-dd9d-529e-9827-5741fea90145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{2 \\sqrt{2}}r^{4} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535343}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89118a1c-8cc3-5bd9-ac73-77a6bba3f97c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^{4} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535323} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ac1d160-d47d-5e69-b9dc-efef92a3beb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535298}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6ab492b4-013c-541e-9b87-6d4016fbd2e9" :question/problem "![](images/66/66c8e80f89c7964cae5443f9e3cb04916ec6aee3e16f27480d8d85d3a344187e.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the quarter-circle shown above. What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_D \\left(x^{2} - y^{2}\\right) \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n$$ expressed in polar coordinates?\n\nChoose a change-of-variables setup with $r\\geq0$ that describes $D$ and the transformed integrand, including the Jacobian." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\leq r\\leq \\sqrt{2},\\ \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\leq\\theta\\leq \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nSince $r\\geq0$, $\\sqrt{r^2}=r$; also $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$.\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D \\left(x^{2} - y^{2}\\right) \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A&=\\iint_\\Delta \\left((r\\cos\\theta)^{2} - (r\\sin\\theta)^{2}\\right) \\sqrt{(r\\cos\\theta)^{2} + (r\\sin\\theta)^{2}}\\,r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^3(\\cos^2\\theta-\\sin^2\\theta)\\sqrt{r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^4(\\cos^2\\theta-\\sin^2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^4\\cos(2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535330 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "026784b9-3bc0-5798-b8b5-2ea62f5c5f83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535297} {:answer/id #uuid "0cad4500-905a-5858-8c3f-a7b60b352b59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535299} {:answer/id #uuid "2eb01dae-041f-5459-8b96-b3482e964c0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535304} {:answer/id #uuid "8e67acde-888e-5817-a582-ac7742afc991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535324} {:answer/id #uuid "f8da406d-b2dc-5b56-b3c5-186cce25d980" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e67acde-888e-5817-a582-ac7742afc991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535324} :answer-field/id #uuid "11f6bfad-4fb4-53b7-b7e6-318260e0f958" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535300}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b24cc741-a0e1-52fd-84de-966d881c283d" :question/problem "![](images/7b/7bd61d778e3ea17a10adad21dd63cfae068af48700e6217194bb48e995c9f6e7.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the circle shown above. What is $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ expressed in polar coordinates?\n\nChoose a change-of-variables setup with $r\\geq0$ that describes $D$ and the transformed integrand, including the Jacobian." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\leq r\\leq \\sqrt{2},\\ 0\\leq\\theta\\leq 2 \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{3}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535332 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49babc88-c8f0-56cc-8e02-d30ec1d26157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^{4} \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535311} {:answer/id #uuid "67e550be-2618-51a5-b473-ae78444d24d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{4}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^{4} \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535319} {:answer/id #uuid "74e8cc33-262d-54d0-9d13-a5424bcf12e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{2 \\sqrt{2}}r^{4} \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535322} {:answer/id #uuid "9424c60b-ac35-5ce4-87fb-8d2efb4c4191" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^{5} \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535325} {:answer/id #uuid "def0eed0-b0f4-542a-9f44-1d63a9047258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^{3} \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535338}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49babc88-c8f0-56cc-8e02-d30ec1d26157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^{4} \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535311} :answer-field/id #uuid "f466e166-f8a5-5be5-ab7f-55508e3ed84a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "be642d90-5a10-50aa-88ca-50cea7daf117" :question/problem "![](images/66/66c8e80f89c7964cae5443f9e3cb04916ec6aee3e16f27480d8d85d3a344187e.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the quarter-circle shown above. What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_D 2 x y \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n$$ expressed in polar coordinates?\n\nChoose a change-of-variables setup with $r\\geq0$ that describes $D$ and the transformed integrand, including the Jacobian." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\leq r\\leq \\sqrt{2},\\ \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\leq\\theta\\leq \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nSince $r\\geq0$, $\\sqrt{r^2}=r$; also $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$.\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 2 x y \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A&=\\iint_\\Delta 2 (r\\cos\\theta) (r\\sin\\theta) \\sqrt{(r\\cos\\theta)^{2} + (r\\sin\\theta)^{2}}\\,r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}2r^3\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta\\sqrt{r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}2r^4\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^4\\sin(2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535337 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "006f8cc3-fe44-506e-abc5-5bcc0cdda89d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{2 \\sqrt{2}}r^{4} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535296} {:answer/id #uuid "3b07f7ba-836e-561a-b89e-43ec179cff33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^{3} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535307} {:answer/id #uuid "41705678-4952-599d-819d-ab0c479baa66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^{4} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535309} {:answer/id #uuid "c81d2b5f-e76b-538a-a273-bbf8662e4bad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{4}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^{4} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535333} {:answer/id #uuid "d42cb897-fc52-56c1-aaa4-b8bdafde567b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^{5} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535335}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41705678-4952-599d-819d-ab0c479baa66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^{4} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535309} :answer-field/id #uuid "20e8a619-79f2-5c97-8bf1-deb805b2a8ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dbec5b4a-692d-58b4-b3b1-bed97fa7c019" :question/problem "![](images/b4/b4a332a1bea58e47ab58fa47c6944213ce4caa5d26e6bfb129fea613adcd524b.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the quarter-circle shown above. What is $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_D \\left(x^{2} - y^{2}\\right) \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A\n$$ expressed in polar coordinates?\n\nChoose a change-of-variables setup with $r\\geq0$ that describes $D$ and the transformed integrand, including the Jacobian." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\leq r\\leq \\sqrt{2},\\ 0\\leq\\theta\\leq \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nSince $r\\geq0$, $\\sqrt{r^2}=r$; also $\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta=1$.\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D \\left(x^{2} - y^{2}\\right) \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A&=\\iint_\\Delta \\left((r\\cos\\theta)^{2} - (r\\sin\\theta)^{2}\\right) \\sqrt{(r\\cos\\theta)^{2} + (r\\sin\\theta)^{2}}\\,r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^3(\\cos^2\\theta-\\sin^2\\theta)\\sqrt{r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^4(\\cos^2\\theta-\\sin^2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r^4\\cos(2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535342 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "490bf319-a19a-5705-874f-041fecb63bc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535310} {:answer/id #uuid "56407af4-1fb7-53b1-86b8-13243e6095fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535314} {:answer/id #uuid "5d545206-ca9c-5b98-9471-863533f19aca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(r+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535315} {:answer/id #uuid "71005229-4609-51a5-b252-aec7c9944620" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535321} {:answer/id #uuid "b12e0871-185b-543d-bc3b-20afa90aead8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(r+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71005229-4609-51a5-b252-aec7c9944620" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535321} :answer-field/id #uuid "51400ed4-9db0-5c43-83b2-f1b92948e695" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f6d7b368-d715-54c1-b3b0-bf00daf35574" :question/problem "![](images/7b/7bd61d778e3ea17a10adad21dd63cfae068af48700e6217194bb48e995c9f6e7.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the circle shown above. What is $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ expressed in polar coordinates?\n\nChoose a change-of-variables setup with $r\\geq0$ that describes $D$ and the transformed integrand, including the Jacobian." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\leq r\\leq \\sqrt{2},\\ 0\\leq\\theta\\leq 2 \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{ln}⁡((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+1)r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+1)\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}r\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535415 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ca1d623-6fb3-5bc0-b77b-f3d7f09c20b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535411} {:answer/id #uuid "22645390-9b31-5c8d-90c3-cc8622341dba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535412} {:answer/id #uuid "5b318775-181f-578e-bc36-15bc47255b14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 16 k z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535418} {:answer/id #uuid "7fabdf3d-f652-5a37-9dd1-4a79130e22e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 16 j z + 2 k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535420} {:answer/id #uuid "d45c9920-d159-56c8-b089-9363351e511d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 j z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535423}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d45c9920-d159-56c8-b089-9363351e511d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 j z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535423} :answer-field/id #uuid "01ad855e-80d9-5f79-b007-f3946d4f9a68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535410}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "45a79900-6a44-513e-8ee7-36f0f63a0a43" :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y,z)=8{z}^{2}\\,i+2y\\,j+k,$ what is $\\text{curl}\\,F?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the curl of $F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\,F=∇\\times F=\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ 8{z}^{2} & 2y & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2y)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(8{z}^{2})]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(8{z}^{2})]k \\\\  & =(0-0)i-(0-16z)j+(0-0)k \\\\  & =16z\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535416 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "363634aa-e8d9-52c3-bf0e-3eda14caeb18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14 j z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535413} {:answer/id #uuid "36ba0c31-5354-56ad-8374-df8a429ac86f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 14 j z + 2 k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535414} {:answer/id #uuid "5056ab9e-f935-5e99-9c47-d970637a2506" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535417} {:answer/id #uuid "789103f3-62e6-55d8-a195-73f4a7b6d1f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 14 k z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535419} {:answer/id #uuid "8da18fb1-ae45-5312-9cdd-f5ffa291065c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535421}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "363634aa-e8d9-52c3-bf0e-3eda14caeb18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14 j z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535413} :answer-field/id #uuid "a01fff05-afdf-5fb6-a143-4ff309517c87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535422}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4ab6255a-2668-509c-9456-b75fdf8371c2" :question/problem "Given that $F(x,y,z)=7{z}^{2}\\,i+2y\\,j+k,$ what is $\\text{curl}\\,F?$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the curl of $F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\,F=∇\\times F=\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ 7{z}^{2} & 2y & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2y)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(7{z}^{2})]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(7{z}^{2})]k \\\\  & =(0-0)i-(0-14z)j+(0-0)k \\\\  & =14z\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536821 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52ceb76e-e2b2-5e97-834d-102232989a20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536425}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52ceb76e-e2b2-5e97-834d-102232989a20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{40}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536425} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0e904c0-c52c-57be-803f-4a9e12590679" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2d7243f0-f25d-51c0-9680-07e1df8e0114" :question/math-academy-id "q-129774" :question/problem "Find the value of ${a}_{5},$ the fifth nonzero term of the alternating series $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.\n$$\n\n${a}_{5}\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the terms of our series $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\n$$ are the coefficients of the Maclaurin series for $g(x).$\n\nPreviously, we found the fourth-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x):$\n\n$$\nf(x)≈{P}_{4}(x)=2-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{4}\n$$\n\nTo find the ${x}^{5}$ term of the Maclaurin series of $g(x),$ we simply need to integrate the ${x}^{4}$ term of ${P}_{4}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{40}{t}^{5}\\,{|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{40}{x}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${a}_{5}$ is the coefficient of $\\frac{1}{40}{x}^{5},$ that is, ${a}_{5}=\\frac{1}{40}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536983 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "237e8e7d-e3db-5f7c-9347-4abfc31811d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536996} {:answer/id #uuid "4f83f8a6-2631-55b9-a475-cf21a17cb881" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{1}^{0}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537015} {:answer/id #uuid "9d691199-900d-5dbc-95ff-fd5da1703bb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}r^{4}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537043} {:answer/id #uuid "b727e49c-dd3f-5d45-b030-cf65baa91799" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}r^{2}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537053} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d59057-b6e1-58d4-8bca-71b7a0d1be8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "237e8e7d-e3db-5f7c-9347-4abfc31811d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536996} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb63a029-3502-5e90-a9fa-8ddfb79c7c2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "00623118-ee32-53b0-8e64-30db43b56fa6" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4,y\\ge 0,\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/d2/d2b3344b7b910f5685d4fd81b75a600608af6b008778ff608cb084edb5a92da9.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(2,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta-2)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta+4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi}{2},\\,0\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x^{2} + y^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)^{2})\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537003 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30aa1de6-9825-58df-a0bb-9361d44a7568" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 5}r^{3} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536998} {:answer/id #uuid "32b99b29-cd14-5ea5-9fc1-15df5b4eb850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 5}r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537001} {:answer/id #uuid "9605e9f5-cd80-5de4-9598-0eef1d67fdca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 5}r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537039} {:answer/id #uuid "9c51e829-74a8-59f0-b024-a19f558413ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 5}r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537042} {:answer/id #uuid "dff20a83-a22c-52be-b879-f991bfe4e845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 5}r^{3} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32b99b29-cd14-5ea5-9fc1-15df5b4eb850" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 5}r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537001} :answer-field/id #uuid "c677a795-89fc-56d8-8a2d-f295aa205e01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3421c850-1e07-524f-a0e9-45c83fcec039" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}\\le 4,\\,0\\le z\\le x^{2} + y^{2} + 5\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}x y\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/ce/cee08173667c9577f4c7aab8cac5386434f2e1ecab8836150ebe8066d1cbaa68.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is a right vertical cylinder of radius $2$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,-2)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=0,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=x^{2} + y^{2} + 5=r^{2} + 5.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's write the equation ${x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta+2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta+4 & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}+4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le - 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,\\pi\\le \\theta \\le 2 \\pi,\\,0\\le z\\le r^{2} + 5\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}x y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{0}^{r^{2} + 5}r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537006 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "321df476-4949-5c09-820e-9305d838bc49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-5}^{\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}}2 r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537000} {:answer/id #uuid "4e59d9d4-da68-5e58-aff1-c4c4a3337e4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-5}^{\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}}2 r^{4} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537014} {:answer/id #uuid "9a68c5dc-993d-52d2-9754-5b09d9106e51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-5}^{\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}}2 r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537041} {:answer/id #uuid "b4961f55-574a-53f6-91ac-244a2907cf1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}}^{-5}2 r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537051} {:answer/id #uuid "c82cc82c-e96d-5c71-9bfd-49718a58969e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-5}^{\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}}2 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "321df476-4949-5c09-820e-9305d838bc49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-5}^{\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}}2 r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537000} :answer-field/id #uuid "346ca32c-f65c-5df0-9d38-5355dcbfffd3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3595f3ec-49d1-5481-82e6-c23464cfa603" :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2}\\le 16,x\\le 0,\\,-5\\le z\\le \\sqrt{- x^{2} - y^{2} + 81}\\},\n$$\n\nthen express the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2 x y\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nas an equivalent integral in cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/57/5764f328a614ddaa96abd28a4568c4cd7dfcf0988033d3122bdce0e1e52b7bc5.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,4)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-5,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=\\sqrt{- x^{2} - y^{2} + 81}=\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta-4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta+16 & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi,\\,-5\\le z\\le \\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}2 x y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}2(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-5}^{\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}}2 r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537019 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30efa546-da45-5a01-977f-f55cd5e7ba61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{4}^{7}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536999} {:answer/id #uuid "3dc4f541-1cf6-594c-ba21-8359f5bbbc24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{7}^{4}r^{3}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537011} {:answer/id #uuid "7a324aba-9b63-5874-a2b1-733a50c7781b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{4}^{7}r